s-standards.ts only has entries for, say, 5 classes but the chain can produce 10+, the renderer's regulatory section would silently produce empty/wrong output for the missing classes.\n\nI can't see the class-standards.ts code, but the fact that the renderer imports it means it's load-bearing. The question is whether the coverage matches the chain's product class space.\n\n**Finding 4: `state.brief.product_definition.subject` — where does this come from?**\n\nThe renderer reads:\n```js\nconst rawSubject =\n    state.brief?.product_definition?.subject ||\n    (productClass ? humanise(productClass) : 'Engineering Report')\n```\n\nLooking at the chain's state construction:\n```js\nbrief: briefBlock,\n```\n\nAnd briefBlock is:\n```js\nconst briefBlock = {\n    original_text: brief,\n    parsed_original: parsedResultOriginal.data,\n    revised_text: anyApplied ? currentBriefText : null,\n    parsed_revised: anyApplied ? currentParsed : null,\n    revision_history: revisionHistory,\n    was_revised: wasRevised,\n}\n```\n\nSo `state.brief` has: original_text, parsed_original, revised_text, parsed_revised, revision_history, was_revised.\n\nThe renderer accesses `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject`. But briefBlock has NO `product_definition` field! The `parsed_original` and `parsed_revised` might have it (they come from runBriefParsing), but the renderer is reading from `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, NOT `state.brief.parsed_original.product_definition.subject` or `state.brief.parsed_revised.product_definition.subject`.\n\nThis means `state.brief.product_definition` is ALWAYS undefined, and the renderer always falls back to `humanise(productClass)` for the title. The actual parsed subject from the brief is lost for title generation.\n\nWait, actually — `state.parsedBrief` also exists (set to `parsedResult.data`). Could the renderer be reading from `state.parsedBrief`? The snippet shows `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` though. Let me re-examine.\n\nActually, I'm looking at the renderer's main() function:\n```js\nconst rawSubject =\n    state.brief?.product_definition?.subject ||\n    (productClass ? humanise(productClass) : 'Engineering Report')\n```\n\nAnd state.brief is the briefBlock which does NOT have product_definition. So this always falls through to the humanise fallback. That's a gap — the title is derived from a machine slug instead of the brief's subject.\n\nBut wait — is `parsedBrief` the same as what the renderer would expect at `brief.product_definition`? Let me check. The state has:\n- `state.parsedBrief` = parsedResult.data (from runBriefParsing)\n- `state.brief` = briefBlock (which contains parsed_original and parsed_revised as nested fields)\n\nSo if the renderer wanted to read the parsed brief's subject, it should read `state.parsedBrief?.product_definition?.subject` or `state.brief.parsed_original?.product_definition?.subject` (or parsed_revised). But the renderer reads `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` which is a flat field that doesn't exist.\n\nActually, I realize I should be more careful here. The `briefBlock` might have been intended to contain a `product_definition` at the top level. But looking at the chain code, it clearly doesn't set one. So either:\n1. The renderer is wrong to read from `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, OR\n2. The chain is wrong to not put `product_definition` at the top level of briefBlock.\n\nEither way, it's a gap. The title is always the humanised slug.\n\nActually wait — I should check if `parsedResult.data` might have `product_definition` in it and that gets set as `state.parsedBrief`. But the renderer reads `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, not `state.parsedBrief.product_definition.subject`. So yes, this is a gap.\n\n**Finding 5: `state.keyMetrics` might be missing `product_class`**\n\nThe renderer (from Engine C code) reads:\n```js\nstate?.keyMetrics?.product_class\n```\n\nBut looking at the chain, `keyMetrics` comes from `deriveHea