\n\nRenderer reads at render-minimal-pdf.tsx (the main function):\n```\nconst rawSubject = state.brief?.product_definition?.subject || ...\n```\n\nSince `state.brief` is the briefBlock, and briefBlock doesn't have `product_definition`, this always falls through to `humanise(productClass)`.\n\nBut wait — `state.parsedBrief` has the parsed data. The renderer SHOULD be reading `state.parsedBrief?.product_definition?.subject` or `state.brief.parsed_original?.product_definition?.subject`.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me re-read the renderer code more carefully:\n\n```js\nconst rawSubject =\n    state.brief?.product_definition?.subject ||\n    (productClass ? humanise(productClass) : 'Engineering Report')\n```\n\nThis reads `state.brief.product_definition.subject`. The chain's state has `state.brief` = briefBlock, and `state.parsedBrief` = parsedResult.data. The briefBlock has `parsed_original` and `parsed_revised` but NOT a flat `product_definition`.\n\nSo yes, this always uses the fallback. The title of every PDF is a humanised product class slug rather than the brief's actual subject line.\n\nWait — actually, I should also consider whether `state.parsedBrief` has a `product_definition.subject` field. If it does, and the renderer intended to read from `state.parsedBrief` but typed `state.brief`, that's a different bug. But looking at the code as written, `state.brief.product_definition` is the path used, and it's always undefined.\n\nHmm, but this seems like such an obvious bug that maybe I'm missing something. Let me look again...\n\nActually, `parsedBrief` is the result of `runBriefParsing(brief)`, which returns `.data`. A \"parsed brief\" likely includes a `product_definition` with a `subject` field. And the renderer has access to `state.parsedBrief`. But the renderer reads `state.brief` (the briefBlock) instead.\n\nThis could be intentional — maybe the renderer uses `state.brief` for the original/revised text and `state.parsedBrief` for structured data — but the specific read `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` looks like a bug.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider. Maybe there was a different version of the code where `state.brief` WAS the parsedBrief (not the briefBlock). The \"unification\" directive (2026-05-19) might have changed the state shape without updating the renderer's read path.\n\nYes, this is a real gap. Let me continue looking.\n\n**Finding: `state.grammarVerdicts` always null**\n\nChain line (approximately):\n```js\ngrammarVerdicts: null,\n```\n\nThis is explicitly set to null. The chain runs grammar gates in Phase 2 but never captures the verdicts into state. If the renderer reads `state.grammarVerdicts` for any display (e.g., a \"grammar gate results\" section), it always gets null.\n\nThe question is: does the renderer actually read this? Without seeing the full renderer, I can only note that the field is in the state shape (suggesting the renderer was designed to read it) but is always null.\n\n**Finding: Worker missing timeout for chain execution**\n\nThe worker spawns the chain:\n```js\nconst code = await runChild(\n    'npx',\n    ['tsx', 'scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx', briefPath, jobDir],\n    REPO_ROOT,\n    jobLogPath,\n)\n```\n\nAnd `runChild`:\n```js\nfunction runChild(cmd, args, cwd, jobLogPath) {\n    return new Promise((resolveP) => {\n        const child = spawn(cmd, args, { ... })\n        child.on('exit', (code) => resolveP(code ?? -1))\n        child.on('error', (err) => { ...; resolveP(-1) })\n    })\n}\n```\n\nThere's NO timeout on the child process. If the chain hangs (e.g., an LLM call times out internally but the process doesn't exit), the worker's `running = true` guard prevents it from claiming new jobs forever. The worker would be stuck until manually killed.\n\nThe chain has internal timeouts on LLM calls (timeoutMs: 1_500_000 = 25 min per call, 600_000 = 10 min for repair), but if one of these times out and the error handling doesn't cause process.exit, the chain could hang