ter.env). The OpenAI key might be in .env.local, but there's no explicit check.\n\n**Finding: Engine B's database dependency**\n\nEngine B uses `better-sqlite3` and opens `~/.forge-truth/forge-truth.db`:\n```js\nconst FORGE_TRUTH_DB = join(homedir(), '.forge-truth/forge-truth.db')\n```\n\nIf this database doesn't exist on the production machine, or if `better-sqlite3` isn't installed, the CorpusClassifier would fail. The chain wraps Engine B in try-catch, so it silently continues without price estimates.\n\n**Finding: `PYTHON_BIN` env var — silent degradation**\n\nEngine C:\n```js\nconst py = process.env.PYTHON_BIN || 'python3'\n```\n\nIf Python 3 isn't installed or isn't at `python3`, the spawn fails. Again caught by try-catch.\n\n**Finding: Class-reference-graph alias map incomplete**\n\nThe chain has a K10 alias map:\n```js\nconst ALIASES: Record<string, string> = {\n    mini_split_heatpump: 'heat-pump-residential',\n    heat_pump: 'heat-pump-residential',\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nBut the classifier can emit product classes not in this map. If the classifier emits a class not in ALIASES and not directly registered as a K10 graph, the K10 shadow returns NO_GRAPH. This is handled (it writes a NO_GRAPH verdict), but the renderer's manual-review badges might treat NO_GRAPH differently from FAIL_SHADOW, potentially hiding real issues.\n\nActually, looking at the code, NO_GRAPH is explicitly handled:\n```js\n;(design as any).k10ShadowResult = {\n    ...\n    verdict: 'NO_GRAPH',\n    reason: `no K10 graph registered for product_class=\"${k10ProductClass}\"`,\n}\n```\n\nSo it's transparent. Not really a gap.\n\n**Finding: `propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign` — imported but I can't see it**\n\nThe chain calls:\n```js\nconst propagation = propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign(design, parsedResult.data)\n```\n\nThis function is called but not shown in the imports. It's not in the import list at the top of the chain file. Either it's defined elsewhere in the file (not shown) or it's a missing import that would cause a runtime error.\n\nWait, let me check the imports more carefully... The provided code shows imports ending before the main function starts. The function `propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign` is called but I don't see it imported. However, it could be defined locally in the chain file (between the imports and main). The code snippet might be truncated.\n\nSimilarly, `generatorSystem()`, `computeDensityTargets()`, `summarise()`, `delta()`, `formatKeyMetricsBlock()`, `parseJson()`, `applyReviewerPatches()`, `REVIEWER_TEMPLATE`, `R4_FACTCHECK_APPEND`, `callLlm()`, `BriefPlausibilityVerdict`, `generateBriefPlausibilityCritic`, `pickRevisionByPriority`, `parseRelaxFactor`, `rewriteBriefWithRevision` — all of these are used but not shown in imports. They're either defined locally or imported from truncated sections.\n\nI shouldn't flag these as gaps without seeing the full file.\n\n**Finding: `TAVILY_API_KEY` loaded but never used**\n\nThe chain and worker both load `tavily.env`, suggesting a Tavily API key is available. But I don't see any Tavily search calls in the chain. The chain's research step uses `runResearchSynthesis` which is imported from `stages/1-research`. I can't see that code, but if it was designed to use Tavily and now uses something else (or nothing), the key is loaded for no reason.\n\nThis isn't a gap per se — unused env vars don't cause problems. But if the research step was supposed to use Tavily for web search and doesn't, that's a silent quality degradation.\n\nActually, the chain comment says: \"R4 reviews from training-data knowledge only. To get real web grounding, route through Google's native Gemini API.\" This suggests grounding/web search is NOT working through OpenRouter. If Tavily was supposed to provide search grounding for the research step and it's not wired, the research synthesis might be weaker than intended.\n\n**Finding: `variation_of` children — dead path**\n\nThe worker selects `variation_of` from