ecFileSync` blocks forever. The chain never exits. The worker's `running` guard prevents new jobs. No timeout, no recovery.\n\nThis is the same \"no timeout\" issue I noted earlier, but specific to the renderer subprocess.\n\n**Finding: `execFileSync('open', [pdfPath])` in renderer — crashes on non-macOS**\n\nThe renderer:\n```js\nif (process.env.RENDER_NO_OPEN !== '1') {\n    try {\n        execFileSync('open', [outPath])\n    } catch (err) {\n        console.error('[render-minimal-pdf] open failed:', err)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, the renderer wraps it in try-catch. So this is handled. The renderer won't crash.\n\nBut the CHAIN also opens the PDF:\n```js\nexecFileSync('open', [pdfPath])\n```\n\nThis is NOT wrapped in try-catch. If the renderer already opened it and this throws... actually, looking more carefully:\n\nThe chain:\n```js\nconst pdfPath = resolve(outDir, 'chain-v2.pdf')\nexecFileSync('npx', ['tsx', resolve(__dirname, 'render-minimal-pdf.tsx'), statePath, pdfPath], {\n    stdio: 'inherit',\n    cwd: resolve(__dirname, '..'),\n})\nexecFileSync('open', [pdfPath])\n```\n\nThe renderer opens the PDF itself (unless RENDER_NO_OPEN=1), AND the chain opens it again. Double-open. On macOS this might open two Preview windows. Not a gap, but a minor bug.\n\nBut more importantly, the chain's `execFileSync('open', [pdfPath])` is NOT wrapped in try-catch. If this fails (e.g., on a headless Mac Studio where 'open' can't find a viewer), the chain throws, exits with code 1, and the worker fails the job even though the PDF was produced.\n\nActually wait — `open` on macOS should work even headless (it returns immediately, the viewer might not open). Let me not over-think this. The double-open is a minor bug, not a gap.\n\nLet me finalize my findings. I'll focus on the ones that are most impactful and not already covered by the 9 gaps.\n\nActually, I realize I should look more carefully at the renderer code snippets to find state field reads.\n\nFrom the renderer's SuppliersPage function:\n```js\nfunction SuppliersPage({ state, project }: { state: any; project: string }) {\n  const suppliers: any[] = Array.isArray(state.suppliers) ? state.suppliers : []\n```\n\nAnd from the renderer's main():\n```js\nconst state = JSON.parse(readFileSync(statePath, 'utf-8'))\nconst productClass = state.moduleDecomposition?.product_class\nconst rawSubject = state.brief?.product_definition?.subject || ...\n```\n\nI can also see the `RenderEngineBClassifier` class which reads from `~/.forge-truth/forge-truth.db`. This is a render-time dependency.\n\nLet me also look at this line:\n```js\nconst subject = rawSubject.split(/(\\s+|\\(|\\))/).map(...)\n```\n\nThis suggests the renderer creates a formatted subject for the PDF title. Since rawSubject always falls through to `humanise(productClass)`, the title is always derived from the product class slug, never from the brief's actual subject.\n\nNow let me think about the `keyMetrics` flow more carefully.\n\nThe chain sets `keyMetrics` via:\n```js\nkeyMetrics = derived as KeyMetrics\n```\n\nfrom `deriveHeadlineFromModules`. The KeyMetrics type is imported from types/module-decomposition. Without seeing the type definition, I can infer from the chain that it has:\n- `headline_output` (with label, value, unit, source)\n- `headline_constraint` (with label, value, unit)\n- `utilisation` (with label, value, unit)\n- `product_class` (referenced by Engine C)\n\nThe renderer likely reads `state.keyMetrics` for the cover page headline. If `deriveHeadlineFromModules` fails (the chain wraps it in try-catch), `keyMetrics` stays null, and the cover page has no headline. The chain continues, so the PDF is produced but degraded.\n\nThis is handled (fail-soft), so it's not really a gap. But the fail-soft behavior means every PDF could silently lack a headline.\n\nOK let me compile my final list now. I'll focus on things that are NOT already in the 9 gaps and that concretely change runtime output or silently degrade quality.\n\nLet me also think about one more thing: the chain's `callLlm` function. It's used throughout but not shown in the imports. It presumably uses the OpenRouter API. If it fails, the chain has various error handling. But what if the OpenRouter API key is invalid or expired? Each LLM call would fail, the chain would degrade at each step, and the final PDF would be based on whatever partial data was produced.\n\nBut this is a general \"API key validity\" concern, not a specific gap.\n\nLet me also check: does the chain validate that OPENROUTER_API_KEY exists before starting?\n\nLooking at the chain code:\n```js\nconst apiKeyEarly = process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY ?? ''\n```\n\nand later:\n```js\nconst apiKey = process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY ?? ''\n```\n\nNo validation. Empty string is accepted. The chain proceeds and each LLM call fails (or returns an error). The brief refinement loop, generator, reviewers, repair loop — all would fail. The chain would produce a minimal/partial state.json with whatever data was computed before the first LLM call.\n\nActually, the brief parsing (`runBriefParsing`) uses `apiKeyEarly` (passed as parameter). If apiKeyEarly is empty, the brief parsing LLM call fails, and the chain throws at:\n```js\nif (!parsedResultOriginal.ok || !parsedResultOriginal.data) throw new Error('Brief parsing failed')\n```\n\nSo the chain exits with code 1, and the worker fails the job. This is actually GOOD — it fails fast rather than producing a degraded PDF.\n\nBut what about the plausibility critic? It uses `apiKeyEarly`:\n```js\nplausibility = await generateBriefPlausibilityCritic({ brief: currentBriefText, parsedBrief: currentParsed, productClass: currentProductClass, apiKey: apiKeyEarly })\n```\n\nIf this fails... the chain has:\n```js\nif (!plausibility.possible && hardContradictions.length === 0) {\n```\n\nBut plausibility would be the result of the LLM call. If the call fails, what does generateBriefPlausibilityCritic return? I can't see the implementation. It might throw (caught by the caller?) or return a default. If it throws, the chain crashes. If it returns a \"pass\" default, the chain continues without plausibility checking.\n\nWithout seeing the implementation, I can't confirm this. But it's a potential silent degradation path.\n\nOK, I'm going to finalize my list now. Here are my findings:\n\n1. `state.brief.product_definition.subject` — always undefined in renderer\n2. `state.grammarVerdicts` — always null\n3. Worker: no timeout on chain subprocess, no recovery for stuck 'running' jobs\n4. R4 \"grounded\" flag ignored by OpenRouter\n5. Engine B skips parts with distributor prices for component_class attribution\n6. Research data not in state.json\n7. Worker doesn't mark failed job on unexpected throw in tick()\n8. OPENAI_API_KEY not validated for Engine C\n9. Double `open` call (chain + renderer) — chain's call not try-caught\n10. `variation_of` never populated through canonical path\n\nWait, let me reconsider which of these are truly impactful and not covered by the 9 gaps.\n\n#1 — This is new and impactful (changes every PDF title). Not in the 9 gaps.\n#2 — This is new. The field exists in state but is always null. If the renderer displays grammar verdicts, they're always empty.\n#3 — This is new and production-critical.\n#4 — This is new. R4 is advertised as grounded but isn't. Changes quality.\n#5 — This is new. Changes cost stack quality.\n#6 — This might be considered part of a broader \"missing data\" pattern but is distinct from the 9 gaps.\n#7 — This is a production reliability concern.\n#8 — This is a silent degradation path.\n#9 — This is a minor production bug.\n#10 — This overlaps with gap #4 (orphan UI routes) somewhat.\n\nLet me also look for one more thing: does the renderer have any \"export\" or \"download\" functionality that reads state fields not in state.json?\n\nThe workspace page shows a download button for the PDF. The PDF is generated by the renderer. So the export path is just downloading the PDF — not a state read issue.\n\nOK, let me also look at something I missed. The chain's state includes `state.designDecisions`. The renderer should display these. Let me verify the chain writes them correctly.\n\n```js\ndesignDecisions: designDecisions,\n```\n\nAnd designDecisions comes from `resolveDesignDecisions(...)` plus any structural gate explanations added later. This seems correct.\n\nBut wait — the structural gate explanations are pushed to `designDecisions` array only when `!allPassed && finalFailedGates.length > 0`. And then the array is written to state. But the `writeFileSync` for design decisions happens BEFORE the structural gate routing:\n\n```js\nwriteFileSync(resolve(outDir, '10-design-decisions.json'), JSON.stringify(designDecisions, null, 2))\n```\n\nAnd the structural gate routing LATER mutates the array:\n```js\ndesignDecisions.push(...)\n...\nwriteFileSync(resolve(outDir, '10-design-decisions.json'), JSON.stringify(designDecisions, null, 2))\n```\n\nOK so the file is re-written after structural gate routing. And the state is written even later:\n```js\nconst state = {\n    ...\n    designDecisions,\n    ...\n}\nwriteFileSync(statePath, JSON.stringify(state, null, 2))\n```\n\nSo state gets the full array including structural gate entries. This is correct.\n\nOne more check: does the chain write `state.acceptanceStatus`? Yes:\n```js\nacceptanceStatus,\n```\n\nWhere:\n```js\nconst acceptanceStatus = allPassed\n    ? 'accepted_clean'\n    : (designDecisions.length > 0 ? 'accepted_with_decisions' : 'not_accepted')\n```\n\nThis seems correct.\n\nLet me look at one more potential gap. The chain's `buildNaturalLanguageLayer` function:\n\n```js\nconst nl = buildNaturalLanguageLayer((design.modules ?? []) as any)\nfor (const m of design.modules ?? []) {\n    const entry = (nl as any).by_module?.[m.module]\n    if (entry && m.overview_paragraph_en) entry.paragraph_en_llm = m.overview_paragraph_en\n}\n```\n\nThis builds a natural language