king at the renderer's main() function:\n```js\nconst rawSubject =\n    state.brief?.product_definition?.subject ||\n    (productClass ? humanise(productClass) : 'Engineering Report')\n```\n\nAnd state.brief is the briefBlock which does NOT have product_definition. So this always falls through to the humanise fallback. That's a gap — the title is derived from a machine slug instead of the brief's subject.\n\nBut wait — is `parsedBrief` the same as what the renderer would expect at `brief.product_definition`? Let me check. The state has:\n- `state.parsedBrief` = parsedResult.data (from runBriefParsing)\n- `state.brief` = briefBlock (which contains parsed_original and parsed_revised as nested fields)\n\nSo if the renderer wanted to read the parsed brief's subject, it should read `state.parsedBrief?.product_definition?.subject` or `state.brief.parsed_original?.product_definition?.subject` (or parsed_revised). But the renderer reads `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` which is a flat field that doesn't exist.\n\nActually, I realize I should be more careful here. The `briefBlock` might have been intended to contain a `product_definition` at the top level. But looking at the chain code, it clearly doesn't set one. So either:\n1. The renderer is wrong to read from `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, OR\n2. The chain is wrong to not put `product_definition` at the top level of briefBlock.\n\nEither way, it's a gap. The title is always the humanised slug.\n\nActually wait — I should check if `parsedResult.data` might have `product_definition` in it and that gets set as `state.parsedBrief`. But the renderer reads `state.brief.product_definition.subject`, not `state.parsedBrief.product_definition.subject`. So yes, this is a gap.\n\n**Finding 5: `state.keyMetrics` might be missing `product_class`**\n\nThe renderer (from Engine C code) reads:\n```js\nstate?.keyMetrics?.product_class\n```\n\nBut looking at the chain, `keyMetrics` comes from `deriveHeadlineFromModules