h blocks that continue without. The result: the chain produces a degraded PDF without any explicit error to the user.\n\nThe worker loads from multiple env files:\n```js\nfor (const p of [\n    resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.env.local'),\n    resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/distributor-apis.env'),\n    resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/tavily.env'),\n    resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/openrouter.env'),\n]) {\n    loadEnvFile(p)\n}\n```\n\nBut if NONE of these files contain OPENROUTER_API_KEY, the chain runs with empty apiKey. The worker checks for SUPABASE_URL and SERVICE_KEY but NOT for OPENROUTER_API_KEY.\n\nActually wait — looking more carefully at the chain's env loading:\n```js\nfor (const envPath of [\n  resolve(process.cwd(), '.env.local'),\n  resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/distributor-apis.env'),\n  resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/tavily.env'),\n]) {\n```\n\nThe chain loads from `distributor-apis.env` and `tavily.env` but NOT from `openrouter.env`! The worker loads `openrouter.env` into its own process.env, but the chain is spawned as a child process. Since the worker does:\n```js\nconst child = spawn(cmd, args, {\n    cwd,\n    env: process.env,  // <-- inherits worker's env\n    ...\n})\n```\n\nThe child inherits process.env, so if the worker loaded OPENROUTER_API_KEY from openrouter.env, the chain would see it. But this is fragile — it depends on the worker being started in the right environment.\n\n**Finding 11: Engine B's `CorpusClassifier` and `better-sqlite3` dependency**\n\nEngine B imports `Database from 'better-sqlite3'` and uses it to look up the forge-truth.db corpus. If `better-sqlite3` isn't installed or `forge-truth.db` doesn't exist, this would throw. The chain wraps the Engine B call in try-catch and continues, so the PDF silently lacks price estimates.\n\nSimilarly, Engine C spawns `scripts/rag/reference_lookup.py` which needs Python and the forge-truth.db. If Python isn't available or the script is missing, Engine C fails silently.\n\n**Finding 12: `state.productClassDescription` — chain writes but state.json doesn't include it**\n\nWait, looking more carefully:\n```js\n;(design as any).productClassDescription = llmEmitted\ndesign.product_class = currentProductClass\n```\n\nThis is set on `design`, which becomes `state.moduleDecomposition`. So `state.moduleDecomposition.productClassDescription` exists. The renderer might or might not read this. It's marked as \"non-load-bearing — for UI / logging only\" so probably not a gap.\n\n**Finding 13: `class-price-bands` and `class-cost-structure` — renderer imports but chain doesn't populate**\n\nThe renderer imports `resolvePriceBand` from class-price-bands and `resolveCostStack, computeCostStack` from class-cost-structure. These are deterministic lookups based on product class, so they don't need chain state — they compute from the product class. But if the class entries don't cover all product classes the chain can emit, the renderer gets null/empty results for uncovered classes.\n\n**Finding 14: `class-hazards` — renderer imports, chain doesn't populate**\n\nThe renderer imports `getClassHazards, computeHazardRPN` from class-hazards. Same concern — coverage vs. the chain's product class space.\n\n**Finding 15: `getClassStandards` — regulatory section depends on class-standards coverage**\n\nSame pattern. The renderer uses class-standards.ts to look up regulatory standards for the product class. If a product class isn't in the registry, the regulatory section is empty.\n\nOK, let me now focus on things I can concretely trace with file:line references.\n\n**Finding: `state.brief.product_definition.subject` — always undefined**\n\nChain writes briefBlock at approximately line (I'll estimate from the structure):\n- The briefBlock construction is in the chain code. It creates:\n  ```\n  original_text, parsed_original, revised_text, parsed_revised, revision_history, was_revised\n  ```\n  No `product_definition` field at the top level.\n\nRenderer reads at render-minimal-pdf.tsx (the main function):\n```\nconst rawSubject = state.brief?.product_definition?.subject || ...\n```\n\nSince `state.brief` is the briefBlock, and briefBlock doesn't have `product_definition`, this always falls through to `humanise(productClass)`.\n\nBut wait — `state.parsedBrief` has the parsed data. The renderer SHOULD be reading `state.parsedBrief?.product_definition?.subject` or `state.brief.parsed_original?.product_definition?.subject`.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me re-read the renderer code more carefully:\n\n```js\nconst rawSubject =\n    state.brief?.product_definition?.subject ||\n    (productClass ? humanise(productClass) : 'Engineering Report')\n```\n\nThis reads `state.brief.product_definition.subject`. The chain's state has `state.brief` = briefBlock, and `state.parsedBrief` = parsedResult.data. The briefBlock has `parsed_original` and `parsed_revised` but NOT a flat `product_definition`.\n\nSo yes, this always uses the fallback. The title of every PDF is a humanised product class slug rather than the brief's actual subject line.\n\nWait — actually, I should also consider whether `state.parsedBrief` has a `product_definition.subject` field. If it does, and the renderer intended to read from `state.parsedBrief` but typed `state.brief`, that's a different bug. But looking at the code as written, `state.brief.product_definition` is the path used, and it's always undefined.\n\nHmm, but this seems like such an obvious bug that maybe I'm missing something. Let me look again...\n\nActually, `parsedBrief` is the result of `runBriefParsing(brief)`, which returns `.data`. A \"parsed brief\" likely includes a `product_definition` with a `subject` field. And the renderer has access to `state.parsedBrief`. But the renderer reads `state.brief` (the briefBlock) instead.\n\nThis could be intentional — maybe the renderer uses `state.brief` for the original/revised text and `state.parsedBrief` for structured data — but the specific read `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` looks like a bug.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider. Maybe there was a different version of the code where `state.brief` WAS the parsedBrief (not the briefBlock). The \"unification\" directive (2026-05-19) might have changed the state shape without updating the renderer's read path.\n\nYes, this is a real gap. Let me continue looking.\n\n**Finding: `state.grammarVerdicts` always null**\n\nChain line (approximately):\n```js\ngrammarVerdicts: null,\n```\n\nThis is explicitly set to null. The chain runs grammar gates in Phase 2 but never captures the verdicts into state. If the renderer reads `state.grammarVerdicts` for any display (e.g., a \"grammar gate results\" section), it always gets null.\n\nThe question is: does the renderer actually read this? Without seeing the full renderer, I can only note that the field is in the state shape (suggesting the renderer was designed to read it) but is always null.\n\n**Finding: Worker missing timeout for chain execution**\n\nThe worker spawns the chain:\n```js\nconst code = await runChild(\n    'npx',\n    ['tsx', 'scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx', briefPath, jobDir],\n    REPO_ROOT,\n    jobLogPath,\n)\n```\n\nAnd `runChild`:\n```js\nfunction runChild(cmd, args, cwd, jobLogPath) {\n    return new Promise((resolveP) => {\n        const child = spawn(cmd, args, { ... })\n        child.on('exit', (code) => resolveP(code ?? -1))\n        child.on('error', (err) => { ...; resolveP(-1) })\n    })\n}\n```\n\nThere's NO timeout on the child process. If the chain hangs (e.g., an LLM call times out internally but the process doesn't exit), the worker's `running = true` guard prevents it from claiming new jobs forever. The worker would be stuck until manually killed.\n\nThe chain has internal timeouts on LLM calls (timeoutMs: 1_500_000 = 25 min per call, 600_000 = 10 min for repair), but if one of these times out and the error handling doesn't cause process.exit, the chain could hang in an unexpected state.\n\nMore importantly, the worker has no mechanism to detect and recover from a hung chain process. No watchdog, no maximum job duration, no stuck-at-running detection.\n\n**Finding: Worker doesn't clean up job directories**\n\nAfter processing a job:\n```js\nasync function processJob(job) {\n    const jobDir = resolve(WORK_DIR, job.id)\n    ...\n    // runs chain, uploads PDF, marks status\n    // but never rmSync(jobDir, { recursive: true })\n}\n```\n\nThe worker creates `~/.pdf-engine-worker/runs/<job-id>/` with state.json, actions.jsonl, brief.md, worker-stdio.log, and all the chain's output files (which can be 10-50 MB per run for raw LLM dumps). Over time this fills disk. Not a runtime output gap, but a production concern.\n\n**Finding: `GEMINI_API_KEY` escape valve never wired**\n\nThe chain has a comment:\n```js\n// R4 reviews from training-data knowledge only. To get real web grounding,\n// route through Google's native Gemini API (the GEMINI_API_KEY escape valve,\n// not yet wired).\n```\n\nR4 calls with `groundWithGoogleSearch: true` but OpenRouter ignores this flag. The result is that R4 (the \"grounded\" fact-check step) is NOT actually grounded. It reviews from training data only. This is explicitly documented but not wired — so the \"fact-check\" step is actually just another reviewer, not a fact-checker.\n\nThis means the entire grounding claim in the pipeline is false. R4 is advertised as grounded but isn't.\n\n**Finding: `GEMINI_API_KEY` / `OPENAI_API_KEY` — Engine C needs OpenAI**\n\nEngine C spawns `scripts/rag/reference_lookup.py` which uses \"OpenAI embeddings client\" (per the docstring). This needs an OpenAI API key. If `OPENAI_API_KEY` is not set in the worker's environment, the Python script fails, Engine C catches the error, and the PDF silently lacks reference anchoring.\n\nThe worker's env loading doesn't include any file that would have OPENAI_API_KEY (it loads .env.local, distributor-apis.env, tavily.env, openrouter.env). The OpenAI key might be in .env.local, but there's no explicit check.\n\n**Finding: Engine B's database dependency**\n\nEngine B uses `better-sqlite3` and opens `~/.forge-truth/forge-truth.db`:\n```js\nconst FORGE_TRUTH_DB = join(homedir(), '.forge-truth/forge-truth.db')\n```\n\nIf this database doesn't exist on the production machine, or if `better-sqlite3` isn't installed, the CorpusClassifier would fail. The chain wraps Engine B in try-catch, so it silently continues without price estimates.\n\n**Finding: `PYTHON_BIN` env var — silent degradation**\n\nEngine C:\n```js\nconst py = process.env.PYTHON_BIN || 'python3'\n```\n\nIf Python 3 isn't installed or isn't at `python3`, the spawn fails. Again caught by try-catch.\n\n**Finding: Class-reference-graph alias map incomplete**\n\nThe chain has a K10 alias map:\n```js\nconst ALIASES: Record<string, string> = {\n    mini_split_heatpump: 'heat-pump-residential',\n    heat_pump: 'heat-pump-residential',\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nBut the classifier can emit product classes not in this map. If the classifier emits a class not in ALIASES and not directly registered as a K10 graph, the K10 shadow returns NO_GRAPH. This is handled (it writes a NO_GRAPH verdict), but the renderer's manual-review badges might treat NO_GRAPH differently from FAIL_SHADOW, potentially hiding real issues.\n\nActually, looking at the code, NO_GRAPH is explicitly handled:\n```js\n;(design as any).k10ShadowResult = {\n    ...\n    verdict: 'NO_GRAPH',\n    reason: `no K10 graph registered for product_class=\"${k10ProductClass}\"`,\n}\n```\n\nSo it's transparent. Not really a gap.\n\n**Finding: `propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign` — imported but I can't see it**\n\nThe chain calls:\n```js\nconst propagation = propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign(design, parsedResult.data)\n```\n\nThis function is called but not shown in the imports. It's not in the import list at the top of the chain file. Either it's defined elsewhere in the file (not shown) or it's a missing import that would cause a runtime error.\n\nWait, let me check the imports more carefully... The provided code shows imports ending before the main function starts. The function `propagateBriefConstraintsToDesign` is called but I don't see it imported. However, it could be defined locally in the chain file (between the imports and main). The code snippet might be truncated.\n\nSimilarly, `generatorSystem()`, `computeDensityTargets()`, `summarise()`, `delta()`, `formatKeyMetricsBlock()`, `parseJson()`, `applyReviewerPatches()`, `REVIEWER_TEMPLATE`, `R4_FACTCHECK_APPEND`, `callLlm()`, `BriefPlausibilityVerdict`, `generateBriefPlausibilityCritic`, `pickRevisionByPriority`, `parseRelaxFactor`, `rewriteBriefWithRevision` — all of these are used but not shown in imports. They're either defined locally or imported from truncated sections.\n\nI shouldn't flag these as gaps without seeing the full file.\n\n**Finding: `TAVILY_API_KEY` loaded but never used**\n\nThe chain and worker both load `tavily.env`, suggesting a Tavily API key is available. But I don't see any Tavily search calls in the chain. The chain's research step uses `runResearchSynthesis` which is imported from `stages/1-research`. I can't see that code, but if it was designed to use Tavily and now uses something else (or nothing), the key is loaded for no reason.\n\nThis isn't a gap per se — unused env vars don't cause problems. But if the research step was supposed to use Tavily for web search and doesn't, that's a silent quality degradation.\n\nActually, the chain comment says: \"R4 reviews from training-data knowledge only. To get real web grounding, route through Google's native Gemini API.\" This suggests grounding/web search is NOT working through OpenRouter. If Tavily was supposed to provide search grounding for the research step and it's not wired, the research synthesis might be weaker than intended.\n\n**Finding: `variation_of` children — dead path**\n\nThe worker selects `variation_of` from the DB:\n```js\n.select('id, brief_text, project_id, user_id, created_at, variation_of, variation_label')\n```\n\nBut the server action `startProjectWithAutopilot` only inserts:\n```js\n.insert({\n    project_id: projectId,\n    user_id: userId,\n    brief_text: subject.trim(),\n    status: \"pending\",\n})\n```\n\nNo `variation_of` is ever set through the canonical path. The UI has orphan routes (gap #4) that might have handled variations, but they're dead. So if someone SQL-inserts a row with `variation_of` set, the worker processes it as a normal job (ignoring the variation context). The \"parent brief composition\" mentioned in the comment never happens.\n\n**Finding: Worker `running` guard — no recovery from crashes**\n\n```js\nlet running = false\nasync function tick() {\n    if (running) return\n    running = true\n    try {\n        const job = await claimOnePendingRun()\n        if (job) await processJob(job)\n    } catch (err) {\n        log(`tick error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : err}`)\n    } finally {\n        running = false\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf `processJob` throws after `claimOnePendingRun` returns a job but before it calls `failJob`, the row stays at `status='running'` forever. The `catch` block logs the error but doesn't mark the job as failed. The worker recovers (running = false in finally), but the DB row is orphaned at 'running' status permanently.\n\nWait, actually — `processJob` itself has multiple exit paths, and the ones that fail call `failJob`. But if `processJob` throws an unexpected error (e.g., out of memory, unhandled rejection in runChild), the catch in `tick` catches it, but the job row is not updated. The row stays at 'running' and will never be retried.\n\n**Finding: Worker `processJob` — silent `null` path for `readFileSync(statePath)` after successful chain**\n\nAfter the chain exits with code 0:\n```js\nconst statePath = resolve(jobDir, 'state.json')\nif (!existsSync(pdfPath)) {\n    // fails job\n    return\n}\n```\n\nIt checks for PDF existence but NOT for state.json existence. Then it uploads the PDF and later:\n```js\nlet stateSnap = null\ntry {\n    if (existsSync(statePath)) {\n        const raw = readFileSync(statePath, 'utf-8')\n        ...\n    }\n} catch (err) {\n    log(`state snapshot parse failed (non-fatal): ...`)\n}\n```\n\nThis is fine — it gracefully handles missing state.json. Not a gap.\n\n**Finding: `execFileSync('open', [pdfPath])` in chain — Mac-only, crashes on Linux**\n\nThe chain:\n```js\nexecFileSync('open', [pdfPath])\n```\n\n`open` is macOS-only. If the chain runs on Linux (e.g., in CI or a different server), this throws. The chain would crash at the very end, AFTER writing state.json and the PDF. The worker would report the job as failed even though the PDF was produced.\n\nBut wait — the worker uses `code !== 0` to detect failure:\n```js\nif (code !== 0) {\n    const tail = readTail(jobLogPath, 4_000)\n    await failJob(job.id, `engine exited with code ${code}\\n\\n--- log tail ---\\n${tail}`)\n    return\n}\n```\n\nIf the `open` command fails, the chain's main() throws, which triggers:\n```js\nmain().catch(err => {\n    console.error('[chain] FATAL:', err)\n    logAction({ step: 'fatal', error: String(err) })\n    process.exit(1)\n})\n```\n\nSo the chain exits with code 1, the worker marks the job as failed, but the PDF was already written to disk. The worker checks for PDF existence AFTER the non-zero exit check, so it never gets to upload it.\n\nActually wait, re-reading:\n```js\nif (code !== 0) {\n    ...failJob...\n    return\n}\n\nconst pdfPath = resolve(jobDir, 'chain-v2.pdf')\nif (!existsSync(pdfPath)) {\n    ...failJob...\n    return\n}\n```\n\nThe PDF check happens AFTER the exit code check. So if the chain fails on `open`, the worker fails the job without checking if the PDF exists. The PDF is on disk but never uploaded.\n\nHowever, this is macOS-only production (Mac Studio worker per the comment), so `open` should work. But it's still a fragility.\n\n**Finding: Engine B doesn't set `engine_b_component_class` for parts with existing distributor prices**\n\nLooking at Engine B:\n```js\nif (existing?.distributor_price_gbp != null) continue\nif (existing?.price_estimate_gbp != null) continue\n```\n\nParts that already have a distributor price are SKIPPED by Engine B. This means they never get `engine_b_component_class` set. The chain's comment says:\n\n\"Engine B — backfill per-line price estimates + engine_b_component_class for every word that lacks a distributor quote. Without this, partVerifications carries no component_class attribution and the cover's per-class cost-stack collapses to {unclassified: total}.\"\n\nSo parts with distributor prices still have NO `engine_b_component_class`. The cover's per-class cost-stack would only classify the estimated-price parts, not the distributor-priced ones. This means the cost stack is incomplete — some parts are classified, others are \"unclassified.\"\n\nActually wait — the chain comment also says the renderer has a fallback:\n```js\n// P6 fix (2026-05-18): render-time corpus lookup for engine_b_component_class.\n// Older state files (produced before estimate-missing-prices.tsx grew the\n// engine_b_* fields) carry partVerifications without the field. When the\n// renderer falls back here it queries the Phase 4 corpus\n```\n\nSo the renderer has a `RenderEngineBClassifier` that does a corpus lookup at render time for parts missing `engine_b_component_class`. This is the fallback. But it's only queried for parts that LACK the field — and Engine B skips parts with distributor prices, so those parts fall to the renderer's fallback.\n\nThis is probably fine as long as the renderer's fallback works. But it's a code smell — Engine B was supposed to set the field for ALL parts, not just estimated ones.\n\n**Finding: `TAVILY_API_KEY` / research synthesis — possible gap**\n\nThe chain loads `tavily.env` in its env loading but I don't see Tavily being used anywhere in the chain code. The research step uses `runResearchSynthesis` which is imported but its implementation isn't shown. If `runResearchSynthesis` was designed to use Tavily for web research but the wiring was removed during the unification, the research synthesis would be weaker.\n\nBut I can't confirm this without seeing `stages/1-research.ts`. I should note it as a suspicion.\n\n**Finding: `distributor-apis.env` loaded but chain doesn't do distributor lookups**\n\nThe chain loads `distributor-apis.env` in its env loading, suggesting API keys for distributors (e.g., Octopart, FindChips, LCSC). But I don't see any distributor API calls in the chain. Engine B estimates prices from curves, not from live distributor APIs. Engine C uses a local corpus, not live APIs.\n\nIf distributor API keys are available but unused, the chain can't get real-time pricing. All prices are estimates or corpus-anchored, not live market data.\n\nBut again, this might be intentional — the chain might have been designed to use estimates rather than live lookups.\n\nOK, let me now compile my findings into a precise list.\n\nLet me also look for more renderer reads. The renderer imports several modules:\n- `generateSubmoduleParagraph` from sentence-generator — this is a function call, not a state read\n- `getClassStandards, mergeBriefAndClassStandards` from class-standards — deterministic lookup\n- `getClassHazards, computeHazardRPN` from class-hazards — deterministic lookup\n- `resolvePriceBand` from class-price-bands — deterministic lookup\n- `resolveCostStack, computeCostStack` from class-cost-structure — deterministic lookup\n\nThese are all deterministic lookups. The question is whether the class data files cover all product classes. But I can't verify without seeing those files.\n\nLet me also check if there's a `state.risks` or `state.hazards` field that the renderer might read but nobody writes.\n\nThe renderer imports `getClassHazards, computeHazardRPN`. These are functions, so they compute from the