he Node.js version. In Node.js 22+, `require` is available in ESM via the `--experimental-require-module` flag or by default in newer versions. But in some configurations, this would throw, causing the log rotation to silently fail (the catch swallows it). Not a direct output gap, but a production fragility.\n\nActually, in modern Node (22+), `require` works in ESM for CJS modules. `node:fs` is a built-in, so it should work. This is probably fine.\n\n**Finding: `state.research` / research data not persisted in state**\n\nLooking at the chain's state construction:\n```js\nconst state = {\n    projectId: ...,\n    parsedBrief: ...,\n    moduleDecomposition: ...,\n    naturalLanguageLayer: ...,\n    briefOverviewProse: ...,\n    keyMetrics: ...,\n    grammarVerdicts: null,\n    brief: ...,\n    designDecisions,\n    partVerifications,\n    partRecommendations,\n    partVerificationSummary: ...,\n    physicsCritique: ...,\n    physicsLedger,\n    acceptanceStatus,\n    savedAt: ...,\n}\n```\n\nThe research synthesis is NOT in the state. It's used by reviewers during Phase 1 but never persisted. If the renderer wanted to show research sources or a research summary section, there's nothing to read.\n\nBut more importantly — the research data is saved to a file:\n```js\nwriteFileSync(resolve(outDir, '3-research.json'), JSON.stringify(research, null, 2))\n```\n\nBut NOT included in state.json. So the renderer can't access it.\n\n**Finding: Chain writes design.productClassDescription onto moduleDecomposition**\n\n```js\n;(design as any).productClassDescription = llmEmitted\n```\n\nThis mutates `design`, which becomes `state.moduleDecomposition`. So `state.moduleDecomposition.productClassDescription` exists. This is cosmetic — for logging/UI. Not a gap.\n\n**Finding: `state.parsedBrief` vs `state.brief.parsed_original` — redundant but both exist**\n\nState has:\n- `state.parsedBrief` = parsedResult.data (the CURRENT parsed brief — may be revised)\n- `state.brief.parsed_original` = parsedResultOriginal.data\n- `state.brief.parsed_revised` = currentParsed (if revised)\n\nIf the brief was revised, `state.parsedBrief` reflects the revised version, while `state.brief.parsed_original` is the original. The renderer should know which one to use. If it reads `state.parsedBrief`, it gets the (potentially revised) version. If it reads `state.brief.parsed_original`, it gets the original. If it reads `state.brief.parsed_revised`, it might be null.\n\nThis isn't necessarily a gap, but it's a potential confusion point.\n\nNow let me look at the worker more carefully for production gaps.\n\n**Finding: Worker doesn't persist actions.jsonl to DB**\n\nThe chain writes `actions.jsonl` to the job directory. The worker uploads the PDF but not the actions log. If the job directory is cleaned up (or the machine dies), the actions log is lost. For debugging failed runs, this means the detailed step-by-step log is only available while the files are on disk.\n\nNot a gap in output, but a gap in observability.\n\n**Finding: Worker `failJob` truncates error log at 8000 chars**\n\n```js\nconst truncated = message.length > 8000 ? message.slice(0, 8000) + '\\n[truncated]' : message\n```\n\nIf the chain fails with a very long error (e.g., a 200KB LLM response that caused a parse error), the error log is truncated at 8000 chars. The most useful debug info (the actual LLM output) might be cut off.\n\nBut this is a DB column size concern, not a gap. Probably intentional.\n\n**Finding: Worker marks running jobs as failed on restart?**\n\nNo — looking at the worker, there's no startup cleanup of stale 'running' jobs. If the worker crashes mid-job (machine reboot, OOM), the job stays at 'running' forever. The worker only picks up 'pending' jobs:\n\n```js\n.eq('status', 'pending')\n```\n\nJobs stuck at 'running' are orphaned. There's no timeout or recovery mechanism. A manual UPDATE is needed to reset them to 'pending'.\n\nThis is a real production concern.\n\n**Finding: Worker single-threaded — one job at a time**\n\n```js\nlet running = false\nasync function tick() {\n    if (running) return\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe worker processes one job at a time. With 60-90 minute runs, a queue of 10 jobs would take 10-15 hours. No parallelism, no scaling.\n\nThis is probably intentional for a single Mac Studio, but worth noting.\n\nOK let me also look for some more state field gaps.\n\nThe renderer has `SuppliersPage` at line 4049. It also likely has sections for:\n- Regulatory (from class-standards)\n- Hazards/Risks (from class-hazards)\n- Cost structure (from class-cost-structure)\n- Price bands (from class-price-bands)\n- Manual review badges (collectManualReviewBadges at line 954)\n- Parts/BoM (from partVerifications)\n- Design decisions (from designDecisions)\n- Physics (from physicsCritique, physicsLedger)\n- Brief (from brief, parsedBrief)\n- Modules (from moduleDecomposition)\n- Natural language layer (from naturalLanguageLayer)\n\nThese all seem covered by the state fields the chain writes. Let me see if there are any other reads I can infer.\n\nThe renderer at line 954 `collectManualReviewBadges()` reads 7 badge signals. Gap #2 covers 5 of them (G1b, G2, G3, G4, G5). The remaining 2 are G0 (physicsLedger ✓) and K10 shadow (✓).\n\nSo the badge system is partially wired. The 5 missing badges mean the manual review section is incomplete — only 2/7 signals fire.\n\nNow, let me look at something specific. The renderer imports from `class-price-bands` and `class-cost-structure`. These likely produce per-component-class cost breakdowns. The renderer probably renders something like:\n\n| Component Class | Estimated Cost |\n|-----------------|----------------|\n| Electrical      | £X,XXX         |\n| Mechanical      | £Y,YYY         |\n| Thermal         | £Z,ZZZ         |\n\nTo build this table, the renderer needs to group partsVerifications by `engine_b_component_class`. Parts without this field fall into 'unclassified'. As I noted, Engine B only sets this for parts it estimates (not those with distributor prices), so the table would show a large 'unclassified' bucket for any parts that had real distributor prices.\n\nBut the renderer has the RenderEngineBClassifier fallback. So this might be OK in practice. It's a quality concern, not a gap.\n\nLet me think about what else...\n\n**Finding: `state.engine_c_summary` — renderer reads but path unclear**\n\nEngine C writes `state.engine_c_summary`. The chain comments say \"the cover-page REF panel renders\" this. If the renderer reads `state.engine_c_summary`, it's available. But I can't verify the exact read path from the provided code.\n\n**Finding: Research synthesis — no persistence, no display**\n\nThe chain writes `3-research.json` to disk but doesn't include it in state.json. The renderer can't display research sources, confidence levels, or citations. If the PDF was supposed to have a \"Research Sources\" section (common in engineering reports), it's missing because the research data isn't in state.\n\nActually, looking at the chain more carefully, the research is passed as JSON to the reviewers:\n```js\nconst user = `...\nRESEARCH SYNTHESIS:\n${opts.research ? JSON.stringify(opts.research) : '(not available)'}\n...`\n```\n\nSo the research INFLUENCES the design but isn't preserved for the reader. The PDF can't show \"here's what the research found\" because the research data is discarded after Phase 1.\n\nBut is this a \"gap\"? The renderer might not have a research section. If it was designed to have one but can't populate it, that's a gap. If there was never intended to be a research section, it's not.\n\nGiven the chain's extensive feature set (BoM, suppliers, regulatory, hazards, etc.), a research sources section seems likely to have been planned. But I can't confirm without seeing the full renderer.\n\nOK, I think I have enough findings. Let me also think about class-table coverage.\n\n**Finding: Class-floors, class-standards, class-hazards, class-price-bands, class-cost-structure — coverage vs. chain's product class space**\n\nThe chain's classifier can produce any product class. The class-* modules use lookup tables. If a product class isn't in the lookup, the module returns null/empty. The renderer would then show empty sections for that class.\n\nThe K10 alias map shows the chain handles ~15 product classes. The class-* modules might cover fewer. Without seeing those files, I can only flag this as a risk.\n\nBut actually, I can infer something. The K10 alias map has these target classes:\n- heat-pump-residential\n- heat-pump-commercial\n- bess-utility-scale\n- dc_fast_ev_charger\n- vehicle_battery_pack\n- vfd-motor-drive\n- auv-subsea\n- consumer_cinematography_drone\n- automated_guided_vehicle_agv\n- autonomous_mobile_robot_amr\n\nThe chain's classifier could also emit classes not in this list (e.g., \"insulin_pump\", \"escalator\", \"industrial_robot_arm\" — these appear in Engine C's CORPUS_CLASS_HINT). If class-standards.ts doesn't have entries for all of these, the regulatory section is empty for those classes.\n\n**Finding: `GROK_4_3` model — `x-ai/grok-4.3` might not exist on OpenRouter**\n\nThe model constants:\n```js\nconst GROK_4_3 = 'x-ai/grok-4.3'\nconst GLM_5_1 = 'z-ai/glm-5.1'\n```\n\nIf these model slugs are wrong or no longer available on OpenRouter, the chain would fail at the reviewer steps. The chain doesn't validate model availability before starting.\n\nBut this is a runtime environment concern, not a code gap.\n\nOK, I think I should now look more carefully at what the renderer reads that isn't in state. Let me focus on the specific renderer code I have.\n\nFrom the renderer's main():\n```js\nconst state = JSON.parse(readFileSync(statePath, 'utf-8'))\nconst productClass = state.moduleDecomposition?.product_class\nconst rawSubject = state.brief?.product_definition?.subject || ...\n```\n\nSo the renderer reads:\n- `state.moduleDecomposition?.product_class` ✓ (set by chain)\n- `state.brief?.product_definition?.subject` ✗ (not in briefBlock)\n\nAnd from SuppliersPage:\n- `state.suppliers` ✗ (not set by chain — gap #1)\n\nAnd from collectManualReviewBadges (line 954):\n- `state.physicsLedger` ✓\n- `state.moduleDecomposition.k10ShadowResult` ✓ (via design.k10ShadowResult)\n- `state.complianceGate` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.g1bManualReview` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.cost_reality_rejection` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.cost_reality_status` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.g3ManualReview` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.moduleDecomposition.g4ManualReview` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.g5ManualReview` ✗ (gap #2)\n- `state.g5UnverifiedParts[]` ✗ (gap #2)\n\nNow let me see if there are other state reads I can infer from the renderer's component structure.\n\nThe renderer has a `SuppliersPage` (line 4049). It likely also has pages/sections for:\n- Cover page (reads project info, keyMetrics, engine_c_summary)\n- Brief section (reads brief, parsedBrief)\n- Module decomposition (reads moduleDecomposition, naturalLanguageLayer)\n- BoM (reads partVerifications, partVerificationSummary)\n- Cost/pricing (reads partVerifications for cost aggregation, class-cost-structure for cost stack)\n- Regulatory (reads class-standards)\n- Risks/hazards (reads class-hazards)\n- Design decisions (reads designDecisions)\n- Physics (reads physicsCritique, physicsLedger)\n- Suppliers (reads state.suppliers — gap #1)\n- Manual review (collectManualReviewBadges — partially gap #2)\n- Price bands (class-price-bands)\n\nFor each of these, the question is: does the renderer read state fields that the chain doesn't write?\n\nI think the biggest additional findings I have are:\n\n1. `state.brief.product_definition.subject` — always undefined (renderer falls back to humanised slug)\n2. `state.grammarVerdicts` — always null\n3. Worker no timeout / no recovery for stuck 'running' jobs\n4. Worker doesn't mark failed jobs on unexpected throw in tick()\n5. R4 \"grounded\" fact-check is NOT actually grounded (OpenRouter ignores flag)\n6. Eng